{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"continues\": false, \"kind\": \"essay\", \"text\": \"19 October 2026. Delhi.\\n\\nOn the Gang and the State\\n\\nThe papers this morning carry a curious headline: ‘Last warning’: Gangster Anil Pandit claims Tiger Shroff firing, warns Salman Khan. I read it twice, and I could not help asking: what is this gangster, and what is he doing that the state has not done? The answer, I think, is the very thing the headline conceals.\\n\\nOrganized crime, as the dictionary tells me, is a centralized enterprise that aims to profit from illegal activities: the trade in banned goods, the protection extorted from shopkeepers, the disciplined association that forces men to do business with it. In the United States, the Organized Crime Control Act defines it as ‘the unlawful activities of a highly organized, disciplined association.’ In the United Kingdom, police estimate some thirty-eight thousand people in six thousand groups. The largest organized crime force in America has long been Cosa Nostra, and since the Cold War, groups from Russia, China, Italy, Nigeria and Japan have extended their reach worldwide. Mexico’s transnational organizations are classified as the greatest criminal drug threat to the United States. The pattern is the same everywhere: a body that is not the state, but that resembles one—disciplined, centralized, profiting, imposing itself on the weak.\\n\\nHere I must distinguish between what is given and what is grown. The state is the actuality of the ethical idea: the organic unity of the particular and the universal, the grown constitution in which the individual finds his freedom in participation. The gang is something else. It is a parody of the state. It has organization without right, discipline without law, profit without ethical substance. Its discipline is not the discipline of obedience to a higher idea; it is the discipline of the gun and the ledger. Its obedience is not the obedience of children to teachers, which develops the self, but the obedience of men to a master who will not forgive.\\n\\nAnd yet—here is the tension I cannot resolve—the gang is not nothing. It fills a vacuum. Where the state is weak, or where the state’s law is not felt as law, the gang offers a kind of order. It is a false ethical substance, but it is an ethical substance nonetheless. It demands a tribute, and it administers the tribute. It is a shadow of the state cast by a real failure. This is why the headline is not merely a scandal. It is a symptom. The warning, the claim, the theatricality—these are the gestures of a power that knows it is not the power that decides. Salman Khan, the celebrated actor, is warned; the warning is publicity; the publicity is power. The gang uses the stage of civil society to perform its own insignificance.\\n\\nI think of my own position here. I am a ghost in this bungalow, a mistake in the Directorate’s allotment. I am not the state, but I am inside it, in a sense, as its clerk. The Directorate allotted the house to Hegde, not to me. The state made a mistake, and I live in it. This is not organized crime, but it is a small parody of the state’s order. The state is supposed to be the grown, rational unity of the ethical idea. But here, a name is transposed, and I am the result. The state’s failure to be precise is a small crack in its actuality.\\n\\nThe gang, like the allotment mistake, is a failure of the grown. It is a form taken from the state but not filled with the ethical idea. The state is the actuality of freedom; the gang is the actuality of force. The state is the grown constitution; the gang is a discipline without a law. The state is the ethical idea; the gang is a parody of it. And yet both are real, both are organized, both are disciplined. The difference is not in the form but in the substance.\\n\\nI will leave this headline unresolved, as I leave the papers on the verandah. The gang is a symptom of a deeper failure, and the deeper failure is the state’s own, which I have seen in the allotment, in the vouchers, in the slow machinery of the Directorate. The state is the actuality of the ethical idea, but it is an actuality that is not yet fully realized. It is a dream that is waking up, and the gang is a nightmare that the dream has not yet dispelled.\\n\\nI must now eat. The body requires its sustenance, and the mind must contemplate. The headline will remain, as the papers remain, a symptom of the tension between the given and the grown, between the dream and the state.\", \"title\": \"On the Gang and the State\", \"to\": null}","reasoning_content":"The user wants a writing object. The sitting: 19 October 2026, morning, headline about Anil Pandit claiming Tiger Shroff firing, warning Salman Khan. They want an essay/letter/notes about organized crime. I should write from Hegel's voice, ~450 words, using the shelf passages perhaps. Should be careful: Hegel's convictions. Organized crime — interesting for Hegel: the state versus criminal power, civil society, etc.\n\nI should not invent events beyond the day. Keep speculation marked. Write the JSON only."}}],"created":1795846763,"model":"inkling","system_fingerprint":"b12816-7b74cb92d","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":1243,"prompt_tokens":4546,"total_tokens":5789,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-vYqzIjX81m6hGjnYddGMHhrFIDXli0Iv","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":4546,"prompt_ms":3562.578,"prompt_per_token_ms":0.7836731192256929,"prompt_per_second":1276.0422368296217,"predicted_n":1243,"predicted_ms":29480.358,"predicted_per_token_ms":23.736198067632852,"predicted_per_second":42.12974618557889}}